Here are the interview questions this topic usually comes up in, from the most common to the trickier follow-ups.

Main question

“What is the difference between Collections.unmodifiableMap() and Map.of()?”

Short answer: unmodifiableMap is a read-only view of an existing map, so changes to the original show through. Map.of creates a separate, truly immutable map that rejects nulls and has no guaranteed iteration order.

Likely follow-ups
“If I wrap a map with unmodifiableMap, is it safe to return from a getter?”
Not fully. Anyone holding the original map can still change it, and mutable values (like a List) inside the map can be changed through the wrapper.
“Does Map.of() allow null keys or values?”
No. It throws NullPointerException. Even containsKey(null) throws.
“What happens if you pass duplicate keys to Map.of()?”
IllegalArgumentException.
“Is the iteration order of Map.of() guaranteed?”
No, and it can change between JVM runs. Use LinkedHashMap (wrapped or copied) if order matters.
“What’s the difference between Map.of() and Map.copyOf()?”
Map.of builds a map from the values you list. Map.copyOf takes an existing map and makes an immutable copy (Java 10+).
“What is the difference between an unmodifiable collection and an immutable one?”
Unmodifiable means you can’t change it through this reference. Immutable means nobody can change it, and that includes its contents.
“How would you make a map completely immutable, including the values?”
Copy it, make each value immutable (List.copyOf, records, String), and then wrap or use Map.copyOf.
“What exception is thrown when you try to modify them?”
UnsupportedOperationException.
Tricky scenario question

“This getter returns Collections.unmodifiableMap(teams). Can a caller still corrupt the internal state? How?”

The answer is yes: getTeams().get("backend").clear() works because the list values are mutable. Then explain the fix with List.copyOf as in the example above.

